window.docContents[169].resolve({"/#open-question":{"contents":"What should merge(a) do when a is detached\nand another object with the same persistent identity is marked for removal?\nSuppose Book has no version or relationships, a book with ID 7L\nexists, and a transaction is active.\n\nBook a = em.find(Book.class, 7L);\nem.detach(a);\nBook b = em.merge(a);  // a stays detached. b is managed.\nem.remove(b);\nBook c = em.merge(a);  // The argument is still a.\n\n\nTwo readings are plausible. The uniqueness rule in §3.6.10 suggests that\nb still occupies the identity and blocks creation of a copy. Read on its\nown, §3.6.9.2 calls for a new copy because there is no managed instance with\nthat identity. To test the second reading, lake run failOnDuplicateIdentity\napplies the diff below to a temporary copy of the model's Merge action\nand runs Veil's finite model checker. The line is the condition for creating\na fresh copy of the detached source e (here, a).\n\n-      require ∀ other, belongsToContext other → pid other ≠ pid e\n+      require ∀ other, e_phase other = managed → pid other ≠ pid e\n\n\nThe original line requires every object in the context to have a persistent\nidentity different from a's. After remove(b), b still\nbelongs to the context and has the same identity as a, so it blocks\ncreation of c. This preserves uniqueness but leaves the outcome of the\nJava call open. The model specifies no successful merge for this case.\n\nThe replacement checks only objects whose lifecycle state is managed.\nIt skips b because its state is removed. The final merge(a)\ncan now create c, leaving two objects in the context with the same\npersistent identity. Veil reports the resulting violation of uniqueIdentity\n(§3.6.10).\n\n\n\nCopyright (c) 2019, 2024 Eclipse Foundation.\n\n","context":"Jakarta Persistence","header":"Open question","id":"/#open-question"}});